(a) Copies of the complaint and summons issued by the county marshal pursuant to this ordinance shall be served in the following manner:
(1) In all cases, by conspicuously posting a copy on the subject dwelling, building, structure, or property within three days of the filing of the complaint;
(2) In all cases, by mailing a copy to the property address by first class mail addressed to the attention of the occupants, if any;
(3) By personal service upon each party in interest if the identity and whereabouts of said parties are reasonably ascertainable and if such parties are residents of the county. Service shall be perfected at least ten days prior to the date of the hearing. Service may be made by the county marshal, by any law enforcement officer of the county, or by permanent process server; and a return of service, filed with the clerk of the court in which the complaint in rem has been filed, shall be deemed sufficient proof that service was perfected;
(4) By certified mail or statutory overnight delivery, return receipt requested, to the most recent address shown in county tax records and real estate records and mailed at least 14 days prior to the date of the hearing if any party in interest is a resident of this state but resides outside of the county or if any party in interest is a nonresident of this state, if the addresses are known;
(5) By publication for nonresidents and residents of the state who reside outside of the county, and whose mailing addresses are unknown. A notice stating the date, time, and place of the hearing shall be published in the newspaper in which the sheriff's advertisements appear in such county once a week for two consecutive weeks prior to the hearing;
(6) By personal service upon a guardian or other personal representative in the event that any party in interest is a minor, an estate, an incompetent person, or person laboring under disabilities. If such guardian or personal representative resides outside the county or is a nonresident of this state, he or she shall be served as provided for in subparagraphs (4) and (5) of this section. If such party in interest has no guardian or personal representative, service shall be perfected by personally serving the county probate judge at least 30 days prior to the date of the hearing which judge shall stand in the place of and protect the rights of such minor, estate, or incompetent person or appoint a guardian ad litem for such person;
(7) By personal service upon the county probate judge in the event of unknown persons or unborn remaindermen who are likely to have any rights in the dwelling, building, structure or property or interest or the proceeds thereof. Service shall be perfected at least 30 days prior to the date of the hearing, and it shall be the duty of the judge of the probate court to stand in the place of and protect the rights of such unknown parties or unborn remaindermen;
(8) By publication in all other cases where the whereabouts of any party in interest is unknown and the same cannot be ascertained by the county marshal in the exercise of reasonable diligence or if any party in interest cannot, after due diligence, be served as provided in this Code section. In such event, the county marshal shall make an affidavit to that effect and serve by publication in the manner provided in subparagraph (5) of this Code section, and such publication shall be sufficient proof that service was perfected.
(b) Orders and other filings made subsequent to service of the initial complaint and summons shall be served in the manner provided in this chapter or in O.C.G.A. Tit. 41, Ch. 2, Section 12, including certified mail or statutory overnight delivery, return receipt requested, on any party in interest who answers the complaint or appears at the hearing. Any party who fails to answer or appear at the hearing shall be deemed to have waived all further notice in the proceedings.
(Ord. of 8-28-01, § 12)

